    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One thing the client was asking about is the \"unexplained\" wear. We've seen cases in other buildings where there are random small chips or scratches in corners that don't seem to line up with standard foot traffic or cleaning equipment. Is there anything in the material properties of concrete or stone that makes them particularly prone to those kinds of random, localized imperfections?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "These random imperfections are often caused by sub-surface issues rather than just traffic over time. For concrete, this could involve aggregate popping, which happens if trapped moisture freezes or if there are localized construction flaws. Stone can exhibit such issues due to internal fracturing or inconsistencies in the quarry material itself. In both cases, the damage originates before the finishing process, making them appear random on the polished surface.",
